[38;2;124;124;124mThe standard approach: Use a Vitali-type construction or a transfinite induction[0m[38;2;124;124;124margument. Actually, the cleanest approach is to show that the collection of sets[0m[38;2;124;124;124mwith the Baire property has cardinality at most continuum (actually it's exactly[0m[38;2;124;124;124mcontinuum since every open set has the Baire property, and there are continuum [0m
[38;2;124;124;124mmany open sets, and meager sets... well, the σ-ideal of meager sets has [0m
[38;2;124;124;124mcardinality continuum). So the number of sets with the Baire property is at most[0m[38;2;124;124;124mc · c = c. But the total number of subsets of R is 2^c. Since 2^c > c (Cantor), [0m[38;2;124;124;124mthere exist subsets without the Baire property.[0m

[38;2;124;124;124mLet me make this rigorous.[0m

[38;2;124;124;124mThe σ-algebra of sets with the Baire property: A set A has the Baire property if[0m[38;2;124;124;124mA = U △ M where U is open and M is meager.[0m

[38;2;124;124;124mThe collection of sets with the Baire property forms a σ-algebra (this is a [0m
[38;2;124;124;124mstandard fact). Let me denote it BP.[0m

[38;2;124;124;124mCardinality argument:[0m
[38;2;124;124;124m- The number of open subsets of R is c (continuum), since open sets are [0m
[38;2;124;124;124mdetermined by their intersection with Q (a countable set), so there are at most [0m[38;2;124;124;124m2^ℵ₀ = c open sets, and at least c (intervals).[0m
[38;2;124;124;124m- The number of meager subsets of R: A meager set is a countable union of [0m
[38;2;124;124;124mnowhere dense sets. The number of nowhere dense closed sets is at most c (since [0m[38;2;124;124;124mclosed sets are determined by their intersection with Q, giving at most c closed[0m[38;2;124;124;124msets, and nowhere dense is a subset of those). A meager set is a countable union[0m[38;2;124;124;124mof nowhere dense sets, so the number of meager sets is at most c^ℵ₀ = c. And [0m
[38;2;124;124;124mthere are at least c meager sets (singletons, countable sets, etc.). So the [0m
[38;2;124;124;124mnumber of meager sets is c.[0m
[38;2;124;124;124m- The number of sets with the Baire property: each is determined by a pair (U, [0m
[38;2;124;124;124mM) where U is open and M is meager, so at most c · c = c. And at least c (open [0m
[38;2;124;124;124msets). So |BP| = c.[0m
[38;2;124;124;124m- The total number of subsets of R is 2^c.[0m
[38;2;124;124;124m- By Cantor's theorem, 2^c > c.[0m
[38;2;124;124;124m- Therefore, there exist subsets of R without the Baire property.[0m

