[38;2;124;124;124mActually, let me be more careful about the number of meager sets. [0m

[38;2;124;124;124mA meager set is a countable union of nowhere dense sets. But not every nowhere [0m
[38;2;124;124;124mdense set is closed. However, every nowhere dense set is contained in a nowhere [0m[38;2;124;124;124mdense closed set (its closure, minus interior... actually the closure of a [0m
[38;2;124;124;124mnowhere dense set is nowhere dense and closed). So every meager set is contained[0m[38;2;124;124;124min a meager Fσ set (countable union of nowhere dense closed sets).[0m

[38;2;124;124;124mThe number of closed subsets of R: A closed set is determined by its complement,[0m[38;2;124;124;124mwhich is open, which is a countable union of disjoint open intervals with [0m
[38;2;124;124;124mrational endpoints. So the number of closed sets is at most c. Actually, closed [0m[38;2;124;124;124msets are determined by their intersection with Q (since a closed set in R is the[0m[38;2;124;124;124mclosure of its intersection with Q... no, that's not right. A closed set F is [0m
